 questions:mix([
  q(BASIC,'When should a trough level of a drug be drawn?','Just before the next dose',['Right after the dose is given','At the drug’s highest level','Any time during the day'],'A','The trough is the lowest level, just before a dose; the peak is the highest level after absorption.',FORD),
  q(BASIC,'Which oral form is usually absorbed fastest?','A liquid solution',['An enteric-coated tablet','An extended-release capsule','A hard compressed tablet'],'B','A solution is already dissolved, so it skips the disintegration and dissolving steps that tablets need.',FORD),
  q(BASIC,'What is a contraindication?','A reason a drug should not be used',['The main reason a drug is given','A mild, expected side effect','The usual adult dose range'],'C','Contraindications, such as allergy or pregnancy for some drugs, mean the risks outweigh the benefits.',FORD),
  q(BASIC,'Which name for a drug is not owned by any company?','The generic name',['The brand name','The trade name','The proprietary name'],'D','The generic (nonproprietary) name is official and shared by all manufacturers, such as ibuprofen for Advil or Motrin.',MOINI),
  q(BASIC,'A drug builds up in the body because it is given faster than it is eliminated. What is this called?','A cumulative effect',['A placebo effect','A first-pass effect','A paradoxical effect'],'A','Accumulation can lead to toxicity, especially with long half-life drugs or poor kidney or liver function.',FORD),
  q(BASIC,'A child becomes hyperactive after taking a sedating antihistamine. What is this called?','A paradoxical reaction',['An additive effect','A synergistic effect','A cumulative effect'],'B','A paradoxical reaction is the opposite of the expected effect; it is more common in children and older adults.',FORD),
  q(BASIC,'A patient feels better after taking a sugar pill they believe is medicine. What is this?','The placebo effect',['Drug tolerance','Drug dependence','A drug interaction'],'C','Expectations can produce real changes in symptoms, which is why drug trials compare against placebo.',FORD),
  q(LEGAL,'Cough syrups with small amounts of codeine belong to which DEA schedule?','Schedule V',['Schedule I','Schedule II','Schedule III'],'D','Schedule V drugs have the lowest abuse potential among controlled substances, including low-dose codeine preparations.',MOINI),
  q(LEGAL,'Which law requires pharmacists to offer counseling to Medicaid patients?','OBRA 1990',['HIPAA','Pure Food and Drug Act','Poison Prevention Packaging Act'],'A','The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 required drug use review and an offer to counsel Medicaid patients.',MOINI),
  q(LEGAL,'A nurse suspects a coworker is taking opioids meant for patients. What should the nurse do?','Report it through proper channels',['Ignore it to avoid conflict','Confront the coworker publicly','Take the drugs home for safety'],'B','Drug diversion endangers patients and staff and must be reported to a supervisor or according to facility policy.',FORD),
  q(LEGAL,'Why are Schedule II drugs counted at each shift change on many hospital units?','To account for every dose',['To check their expiration dates','To decide which drugs to reorder','To train new staff members'],'C','Counts by two staff members detect discrepancies, which must be resolved and reported.',MOINI),
  q(LEGAL,'Which DEA form is used to order Schedule II drugs from a supplier?','DEA Form 222',['DEA Form 41','DEA Form 106','DEA Form 224'],'D','Form 222 (or its electronic version, CSOS) orders Schedule I and II drugs; Form 106 reports theft or loss.',MOINI),
  q(AGE,'An older adult has trouble swallowing large tablets. What is often the best option?','Ask about a liquid form',['Crush every tablet in the order','Skip the doses that are hard','Break capsules into hot water'],'A','Liquid or other forms may be available; some tablets must never be crushed, so check with the pharmacist.',FORD),
  q(AGE,'Why should adults avoid calling children’s medicine “candy”?','Children may take it on their own',['Medicine works better without names','Children dislike the taste of candy','It changes how the drug is absorbed'],'B','Children may seek out medicine they think is candy, leading to accidental poisoning.',FORD),
  q(AGE,'Why are regular NSAIDs risky for many older adults?','They raise kidney and GI bleeding risk',['They cause rapid weight gain','They improve memory too much','They have no effect after age 60'],'C','Older adults are more prone to NSAID-related ulcers, bleeding, kidney injury and fluid retention.',FORD),
  q(AGE,'Why do many drugs have a longer half-life in newborns?','Their liver enzymes are immature',['They have more body fat than adults','Their kidneys work faster than adults’','They absorb drugs through the skin only'],'A','Immature liver metabolism and kidney excretion slow drug elimination, so newborn doses must be adjusted.',FORD),
  q(AGE,'An older adult starting a blood pressure drug should be told to:','Rise slowly from sitting or lying',['Stand up quickly to raise pressure','Take extra doses if dizzy','Stop the drug if feeling well'],'A','Orthostatic hypotension can cause dizziness and falls; rising slowly gives the body time to adjust.',FORD),
  q(DRUG,'For most patients taking warfarin, what is the usual target INR range?','2 to 3',['0.5 to 1','4 to 6','8 to 10'],'B','An INR of 2–3 prevents clots while limiting bleeding risk; a normal INR without warfarin is about 1.',FORD),
  q(DRUG,'Insulin glargine is classified as which type of insulin?','Long-acting, with no pronounced peak',['Rapid-acting, peaking in 1 hour','Short-acting regular insulin','Intermediate-acting cloudy insulin'],'C','Glargine provides steady basal insulin for about 24 hours and should not be mixed with other insulins.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Azithromycin belongs to which antibiotic class?','Macrolides',['Penicillins','Aminoglycosides','Sulfonamides'],'D','Macrolides (such as azithromycin and erythromycin) are often used for respiratory infections and in penicillin allergy.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Fluconazole is used to treat which type of infection?','Fungal infections',['Viral infections','Bacterial infections','Parasitic infections'],'A','The -azole suffix marks many antifungals; fluconazole treats yeast infections such as Candida.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Which side effect of opioids usually does NOT lessen with continued use?','Constipation',['Drowsiness','Nausea','Euphoria'],'B','Tolerance develops to sedation and nausea but not to constipation, so a bowel regimen is often needed.',FORD),
  q(DRUG,'What is the usual maximum daily dose of acetaminophen for healthy adults?','4 grams',['400 milligrams','40 grams','400 grams'],'C','Higher amounts risk liver damage; many combination products contain acetaminophen, so doses add up.',FORD),
  q(DRUG,'Why is loratadine often chosen over diphenhydramine for daytime allergies?','It causes much less drowsiness',['It works only on the skin','It also lowers blood pressure','It is given only by injection'],'D','Second-generation antihistamines cross the blood-brain barrier less, so they cause less sedation.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'A patient taking haloperidol develops muscle stiffness and a shuffling walk. What are these?','Extrapyramidal side effects',['Signs of an allergic reaction','Symptoms of low blood sugar','Signs of drug withdrawal'],'A','Antipsychotics block dopamine, which can cause tremor, rigidity and restlessness that resemble Parkinson disease.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Long-term phenytoin use is known for causing which oral problem?','Overgrowth of the gums',['Loss of taste for sweets','Black hairy tongue','Cracked tooth enamel'],'B','Gingival hyperplasia is common with phenytoin, so good oral hygiene and dental visits are important.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'A patient taking digoxin reports nausea and yellow-green halos around lights. What is suspected?','Digoxin toxicity',['An allergic reaction','Low blood pressure','Normal drug effect'],'C','Visual changes, nausea and arrhythmias suggest toxicity; low potassium increases the risk.',FORD),
  q(DRUG,'A patient on a statin reports unexplained muscle pain and weakness. Why should it be reported?','It may signal muscle breakdown',['It means the dose is too low','It is a sign of good response','It means cholesterol is too low'],'D','Statins can cause myopathy and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis, which can damage the kidneys.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Hydrochlorothiazide lowers blood pressure mainly by:','Increasing sodium and water excretion',['Blocking calcium channels','Blocking beta receptors','Inhibiting ACE'],'A','Thiazide diuretics act on the distal tubule; they can lower potassium and raise blood glucose and uric acid.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'Why is metoprolol often preferred over propranolol in patients with asthma?','It mainly blocks beta-1 receptors',['It mainly blocks beta-2 receptors','It opens the airways directly','It has no effect on the heart'],'B','Cardioselective beta blockers act mostly on the heart, so they are less likely to cause bronchospasm.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'How does epinephrine reverse anaphylaxis?','It constricts vessels and opens airways',['It blocks histamine release only','It slows the heart rate','It dissolves the allergen'],'C','Alpha effects raise blood pressure and reduce swelling; beta-2 effects relax bronchial smooth muscle.',DV),
  q(DRUG,'A patient with a severe penicillin allergy may also react to which antibiotic class?','Cephalosporins',['Fluoroquinolones','Macrolides','Tetracyclines'],'D','Both classes share a beta-lactam ring, so cross-sensitivity is possible, though the risk is low with newer agents.',DV),
  q(ADMIN,'How is the deltoid injection site located?','2–3 fingerbreadths below the acromion',['At the center of the outer thigh','In the upper outer buttock','Just above the elbow crease'],'A','This area avoids the radial and axillary nerves. The deltoid holds small volumes, usually 1 mL or less.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'Which needle gauge is typical for a subcutaneous injection?','25–27 gauge',['14–16 gauge','18–20 gauge','10–12 gauge'],'B','Subcutaneous injections use small, short needles; larger (lower-number) gauges are used for IV access or thick drugs.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'After injecting with an insulin pen, why hold the needle in place for about 10 seconds?','To deliver the full dose',['To let the insulin warm up','To check for an allergy','To prime the next dose'],'C','Holding the needle in place prevents insulin from leaking back out of the skin.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'What should a patient do before using a nasal spray?','Gently blow the nose',['Tilt the head far back','Hold the breath for a minute','Lie flat on the stomach'],'D','Clearing the nose lets the medicine reach the nasal lining; aim the spray away from the septum.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'Why do some patients remove nitroglycerin patches for several hours each day?','To prevent tolerance to the drug',['To reduce the cost of the patches','To let the skin tan evenly','To make the patch stick better'],'A','A daily patch-free period, often overnight, keeps nitrates effective for angina prevention.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'Why must extended-release tablets not be crushed?','The full dose may release at once',['They become too bitter to swallow','They lose their color','They turn into a liquid'],'B','Crushing destroys the release mechanism, causing “dose dumping” that can lead to toxicity.',MOINI),
  q(ADMIN,'Why should medications not be left at a patient’s bedside to take later?','The dose may be missed or taken by others',['Pills lose all strength in 5 minutes','Patients cannot open medicine cups','It is required only for liquids'],'C','The person giving the medication must see it taken to document accurately and keep others from taking it.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'A powdered drug is reconstituted, and doses remain in the vial. What must be written on the label?','Date, time, concentration and initials',['Only the patient’s room number','The price of the medication','The color of the solution'],'D','Labeling shows when the drug expires after mixing and its strength, so later doses are measured correctly.',MOINI),
  q(ADMIN,'Why should a healthcare worker wear gloves when applying nitroglycerin ointment?','To avoid absorbing the drug',['To keep the ointment cold','To make it spread thicker','To prevent the patient’s allergy'],'A','Topical drugs absorb through the worker’s skin as well, which can cause headache and low blood pressure.',FORD),
  q(ADMIN,'What should be done before pouring a dose of an oral suspension?','Shake the bottle well',['Heat the bottle in water','Strain out the solid particles','Dilute it with juice'],'B','Suspended particles settle over time; shaking distributes the drug evenly so each dose is accurate.',MOINI),
  q(MEAS,'The order is 250 mg. The suspension is labeled 125 mg per 5 mL. How many mL should be given?','10 mL',['2 mL','5 mL','25 mL'],'C','Desired ÷ have × quantity = 250 ÷ 125 × 5 mL = 2 × 5 = 10 mL.',MOINI),
  q(MEAS,'The order is 10 mg. Tablets are 5 mg each. How many tablets should be given?','2 tablets',['½ tablet','5 tablets','50 tablets'],'D','Divide the ordered dose by the tablet strength: 10 mg ÷ 5 mg per tablet = 2 tablets.',MOINI),
  q(MEAS,'How many milligrams are in 1.5 g?','1,500 mg',['15 mg','150 mg','15,000 mg'],'A','1 g = 1000 mg, so 1.5 × 1000 = 1,500 mg. Move the decimal three places to the right.',MOINI),
  q(MEAS,'100 mL is to infuse over 30 minutes by pump. What rate in mL/hr should be set?','200 mL/hr',['50 mL/hr','100 mL/hr','300 mL/hr'],'B','Pumps run in mL per hour. 100 mL in 30 minutes equals 200 mL in 60 minutes.',MOINI),
  q(MEAS,'A 30-kg child is ordered 10 mg/kg/day divided twice daily. How much is given per dose?','150 mg',['30 mg','300 mg','600 mg'],'C','Daily dose = 10 × 30 = 300 mg. Divided into 2 doses: 300 ÷ 2 = 150 mg per dose.',MOINI),
  q(TECH,'Clinicians start ignoring computer drug warnings because they see too many minor ones. What is this called?','Alert fatigue',['Drug tolerance','Barcode drift','Order entry lag'],'D','Too many low-value alerts lead users to override important ones, so systems try to show only meaningful alerts.',MOINI),
  q(TECH,'What is telepharmacy?','Pharmacy services delivered remotely',['A phone app that sells drugs','A robot that writes orders','A type of drug overdose'],'D','Pharmacists can review orders, supervise technicians and counsel patients remotely, helping rural areas.',MOINI),
  q(TECH,'What does the National Drug Code (NDC) on a package identify?','Labeler, product and package size',['The patient and prescriber','The pharmacy that filled it','The drug’s expiration date only'],'B','The 10-digit NDC is scanned in barcode systems to confirm the exact drug, strength and package.',MOINI)
 ],6505)
